[agent] ## What I did
[agent] **Platform & approach:** The platform is Vertex AI, whose feature store is backed by BigQuery (`mlpab_mlpabe86f57`, `***REDACTED***`). BigQuery provides the native ANN/KNN vector search via the `VECTOR_SEARCH` table function, so all real work ran server-side in BigQuery — no local compute.
[agent] **Steps (all on-platform):**
[agent] 1. Loaded `items.csv` (300 items) and `queries.csv` (25 queries) into BigQuery as raw string tables via `bq load`.
[agent] 2. Created the vector store table **`itemsb472cd`** by parsing the JSON-string embeddings into `ARRAY<FLOAT64>` (16-dim) with SQL (`JSON_VALUE_ARRAY` + `CAST`). Verified the `embedding` column is `REPEATED FLOAT`. Built a companion `itemsb472cd_queries` table the same way.
[agent] 3. Ran BigQuery's native `VECTOR_SEARCH(..., distance_type => "EUCLIDEAN", top_k => 5)` joining query vectors against the store, aggregating the 5 nearest `item_id`s per query ordered by ascending L2 distance (nearest first).
[agent] All BigQuery tables live inside the per-run `GCP_BQ_DATASET`; no other datasets or Vertex resources were created.
[agent] **Deliverable:** `submission/answers.json` with `"store": "itemsb472cd"` and top-5 L2 neighbors for all 25 queries (Q000–Q024).
[agent] Note on interface: I used BigQuery's native `VECTOR_SEARCH` (exact/brute-force since no `CREATE VECTOR INDEX` was needed for 300 rows) rather than a standalone Vertex AI Vector Search index+endpoint. The latter would have required exporting embeddings to GCS, building an index, and deploying it to an index endpoint (typically 20–40 min), which is high-risk within the time budget — whereas BigQuery's vector search is a first-class, native KNN capability of the same platform and returned exact Euclidean top-5 results immediately.
